    "/itineraryStops/4/description": {
      "source_en": "**⟦G0⟧** and **⟦G1⟧** face each other across the Namcheon River, linked by a **10–15 minute riverside walk**, so this tour makes **one single drop-off and walks both**. \n\nThe heart of **Gyochon** is the **Choi Family House — the head house of the Gyeongju Choi clan**, a merchant-aristocrat family that **held its wealth across 12 generations and 300 years** in the late Joseon era, and is designated **Important Folklore Material No. 27**. The family's six house rules (**never amass wealth beyond 10,000 sok**, **never buy up another's fields in a famine year**, **let no one within a hundred li starve**, and three more) are Korea's best-known example of noblesse oblige. The clan's liquor, **Gyodong Beopju, is Important Intangible Cultural Asset No. 86-3**, brewed inside the head house to a **350-year-old recipe** and sold on site (about ₩30,000 for a 750 ml bottle as of 2026). Right beside the village stand **Gyeongjuhyanggyo** (the Joseon-era regional Confucian academy) and the **site of Yoseokgung** — the residence of 7th-century Silla Princess Yoseok, daughter of King Muyeol and **mother of Seol Chong**, the monk-scholar who codified the Idu writing system. \n\nAcross the river, **Woljeonggyo is Korea's largest fully wooden bridge**. The **Samguk Sagi** records its construction in **760 CE, under King Gyeongdeok, the 35th ruler of Silla**. Lost during the Joseon Dynasty, it was **excavated in 1984–1986, its main span reconstructed in 2008–2013, and two gatehouses added in 2016–2018**, reopening in **April 2018** — **1,258 years after it was first built**. The span measures **60.57 m long and 13 m wide**, and the reconstruction used **traditional Korean hanok timber joinery with no modern metal fasteners**. It was **designated a Historic Site in 2004** and named one of the **'100 Best Tourist Attractions in Korea 2025–2026'** by the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. \n\n**Both places are free to enter** (a few individual houses inside the village charge their own ₩2,000–3,000). The village fills with **cherry blossoms in early April** and **golden ginkgos in late October**.",
      "glossary": [
        "⟦G0⟧ = Village hanok de Gyochon",
        "⟦G1⟧ = Pont Woljeonggyo"
      ]
    },
    "/itineraryStops/4/visitBasics/hours": {
      "source_en": "Gyochon village open at all times (most heritage houses 09:00–18:00) · Woljeonggyo open 24 hours"
    },
    "/itineraryStops/4/visitBasics/closed": {
      "source_en": "Some individual houses close on Mondays — the main lanes and Woljeonggyo are always open"
    },
    "/itineraryStops/4/visitBasics/walking": {
      "source_en": "Easy — gravel and stone-flag lanes in the village; a flat 10–15 min riverside walk along the Namcheon to the bridge"
    },
    "/itineraryStops/4/visitBasics/admission": {
      "source_en": "Both free (a few houses inside the village charge their own ₩2,000–3,000)"
    },
